    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The single decision that matters most is made before you buy anything: whether this deck should be stripped at all, or sanded, or simply repainted. Getting that wrong costs a fortnight of weekends and sometimes the boards themselves.
        </p>

        <div id="why-harder" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Paint Is Harder to Strip Than Stain
          </h2>

          <p>
            The difference is structural rather than a matter of degree. A penetrating stain soaks into the wood fibres and leaves very little film on the surface, so a stripper only has to break down pigment and binder that is already partly held in open grain. Paint does the opposite: it forms a continuous elastic film that sits on top of the boards and is designed specifically to resist water, sunlight and abrasion for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            That film is the problem. A stripper has to soften it all the way through before anything can be removed, and a modern acrylic exterior paint is both thick and flexible. Softened acrylic does not flake away the way old alkyd paint does; it turns into a gummy layer that has to be physically pushed off the wood. Every square foot of a painted deck gets scraped, and that is the part people underestimate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coats multiply the problem rather than adding to it. A deck painted three times over fifteen years has three films bonded together, and the stripper works down through them in sequence. It is entirely normal to strip a section, rinse it, and find a different colour underneath that needs its own application. Budget for two applications on a previously painted deck and be pleased if one does it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a wood-condition issue specific to paint. Paint traps moisture in the boards beneath it, so a deck that has been painted for years is often wetter and softer under the film than it looks. Once the paint is off you may find fibres that are punky and boards that were being held together largely by their coating. That is worth knowing before you start, because it changes the answer to the next question.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE EXPECTATION TO SET</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stripping paint is 30 percent chemistry and 70 percent scraping.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A stain strip is mostly apply, dwell, rinse. A paint strip is apply, dwell, scrape every square foot by hand, rinse, and very often do it again. Plan roughly three times the labour of a stain strip on the same deck.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="strip-or-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Strip, Sand, or Just Repaint?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the decision that saves or wastes the most time, and it deserves twenty minutes before you buy a drop of anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strip if you want to go back to stain. This is the only route that gets you there. Stain has to penetrate wood, and it cannot penetrate through paint residue, so anything less than full removal produces a blotchy finish that fails within a season. If your goal is a semi-transparent or natural look, you are committed to a full strip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repaint if the existing paint is sound. If the film is adhering well over most of the deck with only localised peeling, scraping the loose areas, spot-priming and recoating is dramatically less work and entirely legitimate. A deck that has been painted is not ruined; it is simply on the paint path, and staying on it is cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sand rather than strip on small areas and on railings with simple profiles. Sanding is faster than chemical stripping on flat, accessible surfaces and produces no runoff to manage. It is miserable on spindles, balusters and any moulded profile, which is exactly where chemical stripping earns its place. Many deck jobs are best done as a hybrid: sand the flat boards, strip the railings.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best Route</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Want to return to stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full chemical strip</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stain cannot penetrate paint residue</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Paint sound, want paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scrape, prime, repaint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A fraction of the work</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flat boards only, small deck</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Faster, no runoff to manage</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Railings and spindles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chemical strip</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sanding profiles is impractical</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Boards soft or punky</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Replace boards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Finish will not save failing wood</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lead paint suspected</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stop, test first</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Different rules entirely</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One hard stop. If the deck or the house was built before 1978, test for lead before disturbing any paint. Lead test swabs are inexpensive and available anywhere. If it is positive, sanding and pressure washing are both the wrong approach and there are specific regulated practices for dealing with it. This is not a corner to cut on a family deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Choosing a Stripper That Will Actually Lift Paint
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most products sold as deck strippers are formulated for stain, and using one on paint is the commonest reason people conclude that stripping does not work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sodium Hydroxide Based</h3>

          <p>
            Caustic strippers are the workhorse for deck paint. Sodium hydroxide breaks down the binder aggressively and it is what most products marketed for solid stain and paint on decks are built around. It works, it is comparatively cheap, and it darkens wood significantly, which is why a brightener step afterwards is mandatory rather than optional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thickened and Gel Formulas</h3>

          <p>
            For paint specifically, a gel or paste matters more than the active ingredient. Paint needs long dwell time, and a thin liquid on a horizontal deck in the sun evaporates long before it has worked through three films. A thickened product clings, stays wet, and can be covered with plastic sheet to extend the dwell. On railings and vertical surfaces a gel is the only thing that stays put at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soy and DBE-Based Removers</h3>

          <p>
            Slower and considerably gentler on the wood and on surrounding plants, working over hours rather than minutes. They genuinely lift paint and are the reasonable choice for a small deck, for anyone who cannot manage caustic runoff, or where landscaping sits directly below. The trade is time, and on a large painted deck the time is substantial.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Avoid</h3>

          <p>
            Anything sold as a deck cleaner or brightener, which is a different product and will do nothing to paint. And be wary of methylene chloride products, which are effective but carry serious health risks and have been restricted for consumer use in many places. For exterior deck work there is no reason to reach for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy more than you think and buy it all at once. Running out midway through a section means the treated area dries before you finish, which sets the softened paint back into the grain and makes the second attempt harder than the first would have been.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dwell-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Dwell Time Problem, Which Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every failed paint strip comes down to one thing: the stripper dried before it finished working. Understanding this makes the difference between one application and three.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemical strippers work while wet. As soon as the surface dries, the reaction stops and, worse, the partially dissolved paint re-hardens onto and into the wood in a form that is harder to remove than the original film. A deck stripped in direct sun in August can dry in ten minutes, which is nowhere near enough for paint.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the entire method is built around keeping it wet for the full dwell period, which for paint is usually 20 to 45 minutes and sometimes longer. Work in shade or on an overcast day. Start early or late rather than at midday. Mist the surface lightly with water from a pump sprayer if it starts to skin over, which extends the dwell without diluting the product much. On stubborn areas, lay plastic sheeting over the treated section, which is the single most effective trick available and costs almost nothing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not walk away and let it go longer than the product allows either. Over-dwelling a caustic stripper on softwood raises the grain badly, darkens the wood further and can leave a fuzzy surface that needs sanding regardless. The instruction to watch it rather than time it is genuine: you are looking for the paint to wrinkle, lift or go visibly gummy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Dwell, By Finish Type</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-15 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Semi-transparent stain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">15-30 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Solid stain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-45 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">One coat of paint</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2 rounds</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical for 3 coats</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="working" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Working the Deck in Sections
          </h2>

          <p>
            The mistake is treating this like painting, where you cover the whole deck and then come back. Stripping is a timed process per section, and the section size is set by how much you can scrape before the stripper dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people that is somewhere between 50 and 100 square feet, which is a strip of deck four or five boards wide across its width. Apply to that area only, let it dwell, scrape it, rinse it, and move on. Two people can work larger sections because one can apply while the other scrapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a test patch first, somewhere inconspicuous such as under a bench or at the far corner. It tells you three things you cannot guess: how long this particular paint needs, how many coats you are dealing with, and whether the wood underneath is sound. Twenty minutes spent here reliably saves hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect everything below and around before you begin. Caustic stripper runoff kills plants, etches aluminium and glass, and stains concrete and pavers. Cover shrubs with plastic sheeting rather than just wetting them, wet down and cover any concrete below, and remove or cover anything metal. If the deck is above a patio, plan where the runoff goes before the first application rather than during it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dress for it properly. Caustic strippers cause chemical burns, and the risk is highest when you are kneeling and scraping over a treated surface. Chemical-resistant gloves that reach past the wrist, eye protection, long sleeves, and closed shoes are the minimum. Keep a hose running nearby for immediate rinsing, and do not rely on a bucket.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="removal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Scraping, Pressure Washing, and What Pressure Ruins
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the labour, and technique here decides whether you finish with a deck or with a fuzzy, gouged surface that needs sanding anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Scrape with the grain, using a stiff putty knife or a proper paint scraper with a fresh edge. A dull scraper tears wood fibres rather than lifting softened paint, so keep a file handy and dress the edge regularly. On grooved or textured composite-look boards, a stiff brush works into the grooves better than any blade.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure washing is where most deck damage happens, and paint stripping tempts people into using more pressure than wood tolerates. Softwood decking is damaged above roughly 1,500 PSI, and cedar less than that. Use a wide fan tip of 25 or 40 degrees, keep the nozzle at least 12 inches from the surface, move constantly and always with the grain. The moment you see fuzzing or feathered grain, you have gone too far.
          </p>

          <p>
            The realistic sequence is scrape most of it, then rinse rather than blast. The pressure washer's job is removing residue and neutralised chemical, not doing the removal work. If you find yourself increasing pressure to shift paint, the answer is another application of stripper, not more force.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Max Pressure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tip</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Distance</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cedar / redwood</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500-800 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40°</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-18 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure-treated pine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-1,500 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25-40°</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hardwood (ipe, etc.)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-1,500 PSI</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25°</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Composite</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check maker, often 1,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40°</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12 in</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Expect to need a second application on any previously painted deck, and expect it to be patchy rather than uniform. Areas under a roof overhang that stayed dry will release differently from the exposed centre of the deck. Treat the second round as spot work rather than a full reapplication and you will use far less product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Neutralise, Brighten, and Wait for the Wood to Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three steps people skip, each of which causes a specific and predictable failure in the new finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neutralise and brighten. A caustic stripper leaves the wood highly alkaline and visibly darkened, and both matter. Alkaline wood accepts stain unevenly and can react with it, and the darkening is permanent unless reversed. A brightener, typically oxalic or citric acid based, neutralises the surface and restores the colour. This is not an optional cosmetic step after a caustic strip; it is part of the chemistry. Our guide on deck brightener versus deck cleaner explains why they are different products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then rinse thoroughly and let it dry properly. Stain applied to damp wood cannot penetrate and will fail early, and after a strip and rinse a deck holds far more water than it looks. Two to three dry days is a typical minimum, and longer in cool or humid weather. Test with the classic method: sprinkle water on the boards, and if it beads rather than soaking in, the wood is still too wet or there is residue left.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect before you commit to a finish. With the paint gone you can finally see the wood, and this is the moment to decide whether any boards need replacing, whether nails need setting, and whether the surface needs a light sanding to knock back raised grain. Caustic stripping raises grain on softwood almost always, and a quick pass with 80 grit on the flat boards makes a noticeable difference to the finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think carefully before going back to paint. A painted deck traps moisture and peels at the foot traffic areas, which is why you are doing this job. If the aesthetic allows, a solid or semi-transparent stain gives a similar look with far easier maintenance, because stain fails by fading rather than by peeling and can be recoated without stripping. Our guide on solid versus semi-transparent stain covers the trade.
          </p>

          <p>
            And be realistic about scope before starting. On a large painted deck, the honest comparison is stripping against replacing the deck boards, and once you count two weekends of labour, product, and the rental, board replacement is sometimes the better value. That is a legitimate conclusion, not a defeat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Stripper Dry on the Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chemical strippers only work while wet, and once the surface dries the softened paint re-hardens into the grain in a form harder to remove than the original film. Work in shade, treat 50 to 100 square feet at a time, mist with water if it skins over, and cover stubborn sections with plastic sheet to hold the dwell.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Stain Stripper on Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most products sold as deck strippers are formulated for penetrating stain and will barely touch a paint film. For paint you want a caustic, thickened or gel product that clings and stays wet. Using the wrong one is the commonest reason people conclude that chemical stripping does not work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Raising the Pressure Washer to Shift Stubborn Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Softwood decking is damaged above roughly 1,500 PSI and cedar below that, and the fuzzed, feathered grain you create needs sanding to fix. If paint is not coming off, the answer is another application of stripper, not more force. Use a 25 or 40 degree tip at twelve inches, always with the grain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener After a Caustic Strip</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Caustic strippers leave wood alkaline and visibly darkened. Alkaline wood takes stain unevenly and can react with it, and the darkening is permanent unless reversed with an acid brightener. This is part of the chemistry of the job, not a cosmetic extra, and skipping it shows up in the new finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Disturbing Pre-1978 Paint Without Testing for Lead</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the deck or house predates 1978, test before sanding, scraping or pressure washing anything. Test swabs cost very little and are sold everywhere. A positive result means different methods and regulated practices, and pressure washing lead paint spreads it across the whole garden.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you strip paint off a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but expect roughly three times the labour of stripping stain. Paint forms a continuous film on top of the wood rather than penetrating it, so it needs a stronger stripper, a much longer dwell, and hands-on scraping of every square foot. Two applications are normal on a deck with several coats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best stripper for deck paint?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A caustic, sodium hydroxide based product in a thickened or gel form. The gel matters as much as the chemistry, because paint needs 20 to 45 minutes of dwell and a thin liquid dries long before then. Soy or DBE-based removers are gentler and work over hours, which suits small decks and heavy landscaping below.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I strip or sand paint off my deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often both. Sanding is faster on flat, accessible boards and creates no runoff; chemical stripping is the only practical route for railings, spindles and moulded profiles. Many deck jobs are best done as a hybrid. You must strip fully, not sand partially, if you intend to go back to a penetrating stain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do you leave stripper on a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For paint, typically 20 to 45 minutes, but watch it rather than time it: you want the film to wrinkle, lift or go visibly gummy. Never let it dry, and never over-dwell either, because a caustic stripper left too long on softwood raises grain badly and darkens the wood further.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to neutralise after stripping a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                After a caustic stripper, yes, always. It leaves the wood strongly alkaline and darkened, and alkaline wood takes stain unevenly and can react with it. An oxalic or citric acid brightener neutralises the surface and restores the colour. It is part of the process rather than an optional finishing touch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a deck dry after stripping before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two to three dry days is a typical minimum and longer in cool or humid weather, because a stripped and rinsed deck holds far more water than it looks. Test by sprinkling water on the boards: if it beads rather than soaking in, the wood is still too wet or there is residue remaining.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth stripping a painted deck or should I replace the boards?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a large painted deck the comparison is genuinely close. Once you count two weekends of labour, several gallons of product, a pressure washer rental and the brightener, board replacement is sometimes better value, especially if the wood under the paint turns out to be soft. Do a test patch first so you know what you have.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

