        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Decide the route before you buy anything. Strip fully only if you intend to go back to stain, because stain cannot penetrate through paint residue. If the existing paint is sound and you are happy to stay with paint, scraping the loose areas and recoating is a fraction of the work and entirely legitimate.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you are stripping, the whole method comes down to keeping the product wet for the full dwell. Work small sections in shade, use a thickened or gel caustic product, cover stubborn areas with plastic sheet, and scrape rather than raising the pressure washer. Then brighten to reverse the alkalinity and darkening, and give the boards two to three dry days before any finish goes near them.
          </p>
        </div>

